================================================================================
Title: A Carefully Characterized and Tracked Trans-Neptunian Survey; the
       Size-Distribution of the Plutinos and the Number of Neptunian Trojans.
Authors: Alexandersen M., Gladman B., Kavelaars J., Petit J.-M., Gwyn S.D.J.,
         Shankman C.J., Pike R.E.
================================================================================
Description of contents: This package contains the following files.
  CoorbModel.f
  KRQ11hot_resaei_100Myr_incfix.out
  param11.txt
  param31.txt
  param32-divot.txt
  param32-exp.txt
  param32-knee.txt
  param41.txt
  paramCoorb.txt
  Res11Model.f
  Res31Model.f
  Res32Model.f
  Res41Model.f
  MA11l12h/
  MA11l12h/ma11la.eff
  MA11l12h/ma12ha.eff
  MA11l12h/pointings.list
  MA11l12h/README

Code names: CoorbModel.f, Res11Model.f, Res31Model.f, Res32Model.f, and Res41Model.f

Language: FORTRAN

Code tested under the following compilers/operating systems:
  Fedora Core 3, Ubuntu 12.04, Ubuntu 14.04, Ubuntu 16.04, Mac OS X. 
  ifort, gfortran

Description of code: 
  These models are designed to work with a heavily modified version of the 
  CFEPS survey simulator that will eventually be released publically (rebranded
  as the OSSOS survey simulator) at http://www.ossos-survey.org/simulator.html 
  The model files all function in a similar way. They generate one simulated 
  object at a time, according to some model distributions, and returns that 
  object. Each object will have six orbital parameters, an absolute magnitude 
  and a colour array. The model distributions used here have been found to be 
  acceptable when biased and compared to real detections. 

  The Models are:
  CoorbModel.f - A model of the scattering a<34 AU population as well as the 
                 temporary co-orbitals of Neptune and Uranus. 
  Res11Model.f - A model of the stable Trojans (1:1 resonance) of Neptune
  Res31Model.f - A model of the 3:1 outer mean motion resonance with Neptune
  Res32Model.f - A model of the Plutinos (3:2 outer resonance)
  Res41Model.f - A model of the 4:1 resonance.

Description of input data: e.g. param*.txt

  Each population model needs a param*.txt parameter file as input, as well
  as a random number generator seed. The parameter file allows you to
  change many important parameters without needing to recompile code.
  Common to all the parameter files are the ability to change the width of
  the inclination distribution, the limits and parameters of the absolute
  H-magnitude distribution (parameters allowing for any single exponential, a
  knee or a divot), the width of the eccentricity distribution, the colour
  terms between various filter bands, and whether or not generated objects
  should be saved (use with care - A LOT more objects are generated than are
  detected, so the file quickly gets big).  Some of the models require
  additional parameters in the param file, such as maximum/mean libration
  amplitude, symmetric fraction, fraction of asymmetric objects that are
  leading/trailing, the mean eccentricity (for populations that have an
  offset), fraction of objects in kozai resonance, kozai parameters, fraction
  of scattering objects that are temporarily co-orbital, etc.  All required
  parameters are labelled in the param files.  The parameter files are:
  param11.txt - An example parameter file for Res11Model.f param31.txt - An
  example parameter file for Res31Model.f param32-divot.txt - Example
  parameter file for Res32Model.f with example of a divot H-distribution
  param32-exp.txt - Example parameter file for Res32Model.f with example of a
  single exponential H-distribution param32-knee.txt - Example parameter file
  for Res32Model.f with example of a knee H-distribution param41.txt -
  Example parameter file for Res41Model.f paramCoorb.txt - Example parameter
  file for CoorbModel.f CoorbModel.f aditionally requires the inputfile
  KRQ11hot_resaei_100Myr_incfix.out; this contains the output of the
  simulations from Alexandersen et al. 2013, which was used as input as the
  scattering component in the co-orbital+scattering model.

Description of output data: 

  The models provide a function, GiMeObj, which returns all variables to
  define an object and its orbit: semi-major axis, eccentricity,
  inclination, longitude of ascending node, argument of pericentre, mean
  anomaly, epoch, absolute magnitude, colour array*, opposition surge
  factor, phase of lightcurve at epoch, period of lightcurve, amplitude of
  lightcurve**, a comment string, the number of characters to use from the
  comment string, and an error flag.
  *=(g-x, r-x, i-x, z-x, u-x, V-x, B-x, R-x, I-x, where x is whichever band
  you prefer/define in the param file - colours for bands that no survey was
  conducted in are irrelevant) **=to ignore lightcurve effects, simply set
  the amplitude to zero in the model (don't set the period to 0).

  Calling sequence: 
  call GiMeObj(filena, seed, a, e, inc, node, peri, M, epoch, h, color, gb, ph,
  c  period, amp, comp, ierr) filena is the filename of the parameter file, 
  seed is a random number generator seed, and the rest are the variables 
  described above in the description of output data (in same order). 
  
System requirements: A Fortran compiler, some kind of text editor. 

Calls to external routines: pos_cart converts orbital elements to cartesian 
  coordinates.  zero2pi converts a number to be in the range 0 to 2*pi (so 
  is the same as the number modulo 2*pi) incdism returns a random number from 
  a distribution as defined by the parameters passed into it.  We do not have 
  the rights to publically release these functions, however they are all very 
  simple functions that can easily be substituted. The functions used here 
  will be in the OSSOS survey simulator release.  

Additional comments: These are the model scripts used in
  Alexandersen et al. 2016 to simulate survey observations of various
  populations. The .f files are the model scripts which generate simulated
  objects, which are passed to the survey simulator to test whether
  they are detectable. The param*.txt files are parameter files read by the
  model (allowing multiple parametrically similar models to be tested without
  recompiling). 
  KRQ11hot_resaei_100Myr_incfix.out is the output of the simulations from
  Alexandersen et al. 2013, which was used as input as the scattering component
  in the co-orbital+scattering model.

